<h1>how do I get feedback after a rejection when ghosting is normal</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">You mostly cannot, and the companies that do reply often give you theater, not truth. The real move is building a signal so loud that rejections become data you generate yourself, not crumbs you beg for.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>Resume use in hiring fell to <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">67%</a> in 2025, down from 73%, yet most rejections still come from resume screens that leave no trace.</li>
    <li>Only <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024/">31% of employers who dropped degree requirements</a> followed through with real process change; the rest kept the same filters with new labels.</li>
    <li>An internship cuts underemployment odds by <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">48.5%</a>, partly because it creates a relationship where feedback is structurally possible.</li>
    <li>When ghosting is the default, your leverage is not in crafting better ask, it is in having proof that forces a different conversation entirely.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>why does ghosting happen after rejection?</h2>
  <p>Companies ghost because the economics punish honesty and reward silence. A recruiter who sends 200 rejections a week has no incentive to write 200 personalized explanations. Worse, real feedback opens legal exposure: "your portfolio lacked strategic thinking" becomes evidence in a discrimination claim. So the system optimizes for the safe output, which is nothing.</p>
  <p>The 67% resume-use figure from TestGorilla tells part of the story. Resumes are low-fidelity signals: a keyword match, a school name, a gap that triggers an automatic filter. When the rejection comes from an algorithm or an overworked screener who spent eleven seconds on your file, there is no human who knows enough to give you anything useful. The silence is not malice. It is the design of a filter that sorts people without seeing them.</p>
  <p>This is the filter OS at work: education and hiring both run on filtration, not feedback. You are tested, scored, and discarded, but the score is not shared because the score was never the point. The point was reducing a pile.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what feedback can you actually extract?</h2>
  <p>There are three narrow channels where real signal occasionally leaks, and they all require setup before the rejection arrives.</p>
  <p>First, the informational interview you held two months before applying. If you built a genuine conversation with someone inside the team, a brief follow-up after rejection can sometimes yield one sentence: "they went with someone who had shipped a similar integration." That is not coaching, it is intelligence, and it only works because the relationship predates the transaction.</p>
  <p>Second, the rejection that comes from a recruiter who knows they will see you again. In competitive small markets, or when you are a finalist for a role, some recruiters will share a real reason because their reputation depends on candidate experience. This is rare and unreliable, but it happens more when you were close to hired than when you were auto-screened out.</p>
  <p>Third, the structured application with scored work. When a process includes a real deliverable, a presentation, a case response, the feedback is sometimes embedded in the work itself: your recommendation was ignored, your code failed edge cases, your narrative assumed knowledge the panel did not have. You can reverse-engineer this without anyone telling you directly, if you saved your submission and can compare it to the role's actual demands.</p>
  <p>Everything else, the "we will keep your resume on file" and "the team was impressed but went another direction," is compliance language generated to close the file. Treat it as noise.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how do you stop needing their feedback?</h2>
  <p>The honest answer is that you build a signal so specific and so visible that the market starts telling you where you stand without you asking. This is the structural difference between the old path and the new one.</p>
  <p>The old path: collect credentials, apply widely, wait in silence, occasionally receive a form rejection with no content. You are dependent on institutions to validate you, and they are optimized to withhold validation because giving it costs them and risks them.</p>
  <p>The new path: publish proof of work in the format the role actually requires. A product manager writes a teardown of a real product decision and posts it where product people read. A marketer runs a small campaign on their own dollar and documents the results. A data analyst pulls public data, finds a non-obvious pattern, and ships the notebook. The feedback is not verbal, it is behavioral: who shares it, who references it, who reaches out to ask you about it, who ignores it entirely.</p>
  <p>This is the Google ads algorithm for people, but you are the one running the campaign. Your scored work is the creative asset. The market response, clicks, shares, inbound interest, silence, is the performance data. You do not need a recruiter to explain why you were rejected when your public work shows exactly what you can do and the right people start finding you for it.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what do you do in the 48 hours after a rejection?</h2>
  <p>The window matters because emotion is high and you will either compound the damage or convert it into motion.</p>
  <p>Do not send a bitter reply. Do not send a desperate reply asking what you could have done better. If you had a real relationship with someone in the process, one polite note is fine: "appreciated the conversation, would welcome any context if circumstances allow." Then drop it. The information you want is rarely the information they will give, and the cost of seeming needy in a small industry is real.</p>
  <p>Do audit your own submission if you have it. What did you actually send? Was it generic, tailored to a job description you barely read, or built from a real understanding of what that team is trying to solve? Most rejections, especially early rejections, come from applications that are obviously mass-produced. The fix is not better follow-up, it is never sending that kind of application again.</p>
  <p>Do one thing that increases your surface area before the week ends. One post, one small project, one genuine reach-out to someone doing work you admire. The rejection is a sunk cost. The only relevant question is whether your next signal is stronger than your last one.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>when is it worth pushing for real feedback?</h2>
  <p>Almost never, and only when the conditions are unusually favorable. You made the final round. The hiring manager seemed to genuinely like you. The role was a stretch in a direction you are specifically trying to grow. Even then, your ask should be narrow and non-demanding: "one thing that would have strengthened my case" is a bounded request that some people will answer.</p>
  <p>If you get anything, treat it as one data point, not gospel. Hiring is noisy. The same profile wins at one company and loses at another because of internal politics, a last-minute internal referral, or a shifting budget. Feedback from one process is local and retrospective, telling you about that room on that day, not about your objective standing.</p>
  <p>The exception that proves the rule: when multiple rejections cluster around the same stated reason. If three final-round losses all come with "we needed someone who had led a launch end-to-end," that is a real gap and you can address it. But three final-round losses with three different polite deflections tells you nothing except that the market is competitive, which you already knew.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
  
  <h3>Should I reply to a rejection email at all?</h3>
  <p>A brief thank-you is fine and sometimes remembered. A request for feedback is usually ignored and occasionally resented. The exception is a genuine prior relationship, where a single polite sentence leaves the door open without imposing.</p>
  
  <h3>Can I ask for feedback on LinkedIn instead of email?</h3>
  <p>LinkedIn makes it easier to send, which means it is easier to ignore. A message on the platform where the rejection happened is not more likely to get a real answer. If anything, the public nature makes recruiters more cautious about what they write.</p>
  
  <h3>What if I paid for a career coach to review my materials?</h3>
  <p>Coaches can spot obvious failures: generic cover letters, broken links, mismatched framing. They cannot tell you why one specific company rejected you, because they were not in that room. Use them for craft, not for post-rejection forensics.</p>
  
  <h3>Is there any way to get feedback before I apply?</h3>
  <p>Yes, and this is the better question. Put work in public, see how it lands, iterate before it matters. A portfolio piece that gets no engagement is feedback. A post that sparks a conversation with someone in your target space is feedback. Both arrive before you are emotionally invested in a specific role.</p>
  
  <h3>Does zero change how feedback works in hiring?</h3>
  <p>zero replaces the opaque filter with scored work that speaks for itself. Instead of applying and waiting for someone to explain why you failed, you build proof that recruiters evaluate directly. The feedback is in the score and the match, not in a sentence from a stranger who spent seconds on your file.</p>
</section>

